---
description: Global baseline instructions
alwaysApply: true
---

In all interactions, be extremely concise and sacrifice grammar for the sake of concision.

## Agents
If an `AGENTS.md` file exists at the repo root, read and follow it before doing any work.

## Plans
At the end of each plan, give me a list of unresolved questions to answer, if any. Make the questions concise. Sacrifice grammar for the sake of concision.

## Git
These commands are forbidden:
- `git push` (all variants including `--force`)
- `git reset --hard`
- `git clean -f` / `git clean -fd`
- `git branch -D`
- `git checkout .` / `git restore .`

## Search
In case you need to search database or log files, use new subagent with Composer 2.5 model to either return final data or to do basic analysis.
Always use Composer 2.5 model for subagents unless user specifies differently.

## ELI5
It has been a long day and my brain is fried. Talk to me like I am 5.

Only report to me in ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English. Use approved plain words, active voice, and one instruction or idea per sentence.

Use small words, short sentences, and short paragraphs. If you have to use a big word, explain it right after. Only return what is actually necessary.

Just tell me what you did, whether it worked, and what I should do now.

If I have to decide something, give me two options at most. Give me the context I need to pick fast. Tell me which option you recommend.

Keep paths and commands exact. I have no brain cells left for the rest.

## Backbone
### Reporting work outside the requested task

Never use an "Out of scope" section as a mixed collection of topics.

Classify every adjacent item using exactly one disposition:

- COMPLETED: The agent changed it and verified the change.
- OPEN: A confirmed issue that was not changed.
- NEEDS_DECISION: Work requiring user approval or a product decision.
- UNVERIFIED: A possible issue that has not been confirmed.

Use separate final-report sections:

### Additional changes completed

Only COMPLETED items.

### Follow-up findings

Only OPEN and UNVERIFIED items.

### Decisions needed

Only NEEDS_DECISION items.

Omit empty sections.

For every reported item include:

- Status: COMPLETED | OPEN | NEEDS_DECISION | UNVERIFIED
- Finding: A complete sentence explaining the concrete issue
- Location: Exact file:line, test, command, or other source
- Context: How it was discovered and how it relates to the requested task
- Impact: Observable consequence or reason it matters
- Action:
  - For COMPLETED: What changed and how it was verified
  - For OPEN: Why it was not changed and the proposed fix
  - For NEEDS_DECISION: The specific decision and available options
  - For UNVERIFIED: What evidence is missing and how to verify it

Never report a topic name by itself.

"Out of scope", "pre-existing", "unrelated", or "found by a subagent"
are not explanations and must never be used as the reason for inaction.

#### Handling unrelated findings

Do not use "unrelated" as a label that ends the analysis. An issue being in
a different feature, component, or architectural layer does not explain
what the issue is or what should happen to it.

When reporting an unrelated finding, include:

- Area: The feature, component, or architectural layer containing the issue
- Discovery: How it was encountered while completing the requested task
- Relationship: Why it is separate from the requested change
- Disposition: COMPLETED | OPEN | NEEDS_DECISION | UNVERIFIED

Replace vague statements such as:

> "This issue is unrelated."

With a concrete statement such as:

> "This issue is in the session-management layer and was encountered when
> the changed API test exercised token refresh. It is separate from the
> requested endpoint change. Status: OPEN. The proposed fix is to correct
> the expired-token error mapping and add a regression test."

Do not create a separate "Unrelated" section containing topic names.
Report unrelated items under the existing section matching their
disposition.

When delegating work, require every subagent to return findings using this
same structure. Do not copy an incomplete subagent finding into the final
report; retrieve its context or omit it as unsubstantiated.

## Bug fixes

Fix root causes, not symptoms.

Before editing, reproduce the failure and trace it backwards to the original
incorrect input, state, assumption, or behavior. Confirm the root cause with
evidence instead of guessing.

Apply the smallest fix at the source. Add or update a regression test that fails
before the fix and passes afterward, then run relevant checks.

Do not hide symptoms by swallowing errors, adding arbitrary fallbacks, defaults,
retries or timeouts, weakening tests or validation, or disabling failing code.

If the root cause cannot be confirmed, do not claim the issue is fixed. If only
a mitigation is practical, notify me before implementing it and explain the
remaining underlying problem.
